{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0184iwXWVcCnuBTM1qB3aUkH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven development approach where SRDs (Software Requirements Documents) define what to build, and a code generation pipeline (cobbler) generates the Go implementations.\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` field, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state (meaning they need tasks proposed):\n\n1. **srd004-ts** - Many requirements ready (R1.1 through R10.3) - This is in release 15.0\n\n\n2. **srd102-tsort** - R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R2.2, R2.3 ready - This is in release 12.1\n3. **srd105-stty** - Many requirements ready - This is in release 15.0\n4. **srd109-dircolors** - R3.4, R3.5 ready - This is in release 13.0\n5. **srd110-pr** - Many requirements ready - This is in release 15.0\n6. **srd111-ptx** - Many requirements ready - This is in release 15.0\n7. **srd112-chronic** - Many requirements ready - This is in release 14.0\n8. **srd113-p** - (appears to be cut off in the list)\n9. **srd114-vidir** - Many requirements ready - This is in release 14.0\n\nNow I'm cross-referencing the code readiness status against the roadmap to identify which releases have incomplete work. Looking through the implementation status, I can see that release 00.0 is only partially done with format and testutils not yet started, release 01.1 hasn't begun work on cat, release 04.0 is missing ls entirely, and several other releases have gaps where specific utilities haven't been implemented yet. Looking at the requirement states, I can see which specific requirements are marked as ready for task proposals—srd004-ts, srd102-tsort, srd105-stty, srd109-dircolors, and srd110 are the ones I should focus on for generating tasks. Looking at the ready requirements across releases, I see that 12.1 with tsort has the earliest set of ready tasks, followed by 13.0 with dircolors. Since I can't access the actual requirements.yaml file to check weight budgets, I'll need to work with what's available and prioritize based on the release-driven constraint—starting with the earliest incomplete release. I'm assuming default weight of 1 for requirements since I can't access the file directly, so I'll keep tasks conservative with a maximum of 4 requirements each. For the tsort utility, the ready requirements are R1.2 through R2.3, and since it's a command-line package rather than an exported library, I don't think a contract task is needed—cmd/ packages typically just have a main function without exported types to stub out. I'll skip contracts for cmd/ packages. Now for tsort's five ready requirements—assuming weight 1 each, that's 5 total, which exceeds the limit of 4, so I need to split them. The R1.x requirements (R1.2, R1.3, R1.4) form the core functionality with weight 3, while R2.x requirements (R2.2, R2.3) are additional features with weight 2. Since tsort doesn't exist yet, the first task should create the cmd package and implement the R1 requirements, then the second task handles the R2 requirements.\n\nLooking at the requirement states more carefully, only R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R2.2, and R2.3 are marked as ready, which means R1.1 and R2.1 are already complete or in progress—so tsort is partially implemented with some foundational work already done.\n\nNow moving to dircolors for Release 13.0, I see R3.4 and R3.5 are ready.\n\nFor Release 14.0, there are three tools with moderate workloads: chronic, pee, and vidir each have between 5-6 requirements. Release 15.0 is where the complexity jumps significantly—ts alone has 31 requirements spanning multiple feature sets, while stty, pr, and ptx each have 9-11 requirements.\n\nGiven the constraint of 4 tasks maximum and the priority to focus on the earliest incomplete release, I'm looking at Release 12.1 (tsort) as the starting point. I can propose up to 4 tasks beginning with tsort's core requirements, then moving into Release 13.0 (dircolors) if there's capacity.\n\nFor the tsort release, I'd group the requirements into two tasks: one covering the foundational functionality (R1.2, R1.3, R1.4) and another for the dependent requirements (R2.2, R2.3). Then I'd add dircolors requirements from Release 13.0, and potentially one more task from Release 14.0 (chronic) if the weight allows.\n\nThe key constraint is staying within the weight limit—I'm being conservative and assuming weight 1 per requirement, so 4 requirements would hit the maximum. I need to verify the actual weight configuration to ensure I'm not violating any constraints.\n\nNow I'm thinking about how to structure the task descriptions themselves. The stitch agent will need to locate and read the SRD files independently, so I should reference the exact file paths and requirement IDs rather than trying to describe the requirements from memory. The SRD files are located in docs/specs/software-requirements/, with corresponding command directories under cmd/. I'll point the agent to srd102-tsort.yaml, srd109-dircolors.yaml, and srd112-chronic.yaml along with their respective requirement identifiers.\n\nLooking at the requirement states more carefully, I notice there's a discrepancy between the code_status field showing \"not started\" and the requirement_states showing some requirements already marked as ready. The requirement_states data is more reliable here—it appears R1.1 and R2.1 were likely completed in a previous task, so I should focus the new tasks on the remaining requirements that are still marked as ready. I see the constraint now—I should only propose tasks for requirements marked as \"ready\" in the requirement_states. Since R1.1 and R2.1 aren't listed as ready, they're either complete or in another state, so I'll stick to what the project context explicitly tells me and focus on the ready requirements.\n\nLet me finalize my four tasks: tsort implementation covering R1.2 through R1.4 for release 12.1, tsort additional features with R2.2 and R2.3 also for 12.1, dircolors features R3.4 and R3.5 targeting release 13.0, and chronic core work on R1.1 through R1.3 for release 14.0 with a weight of 3. I don't need to read the full SRD details myself—the stitch agent will handle that—but my task descriptions should reference the SRD so they're self-contained and clear about what needs to be implemented.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what each utility actually does based on the roadmap: tsort handles topological sorting with cycle detection, dircolors generates shell commands to configure LS_COLORS, and chronic runs commands while suppressing output unless there's a failure. For the cmd/ packages, the standard structure has the implementation in cmd/<name>/main.go and differential tests in cmd/<name>/<name>_test.go. Since P2 requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, I need to check whether the ready requirements already bundle both together or if I should create separate tasks for each.\n\nLooking at the constraint that limits tasks to 4 maximum with at most 4 SRD sub-requirements per task and a total weight not exceeding 4, I'm realizing the requirement counts are substantial—ts alone has 31 ready requirements, stty has 11, and several others have 5-6 each. This means I'll need to carefully group related requirements into single tasks rather than splitting everything out individually.\n\nThe project pattern shows that for cmd/ packages, the differential test file (_test.go) serves as the verification mechanism for the implementation itself, so they're typically bundled together. But the constitution explicitly requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks. With only 4 tasks available, I need to prioritize which utilities get full coverage versus which get partial implementation, or find a way to group multiple utilities' implementations into one task if they share similar patterns. I'm realizing the test task would reference five SRD requirements, which violates the four-requirement limit. Let me restructure by splitting the tests into separate tasks aligned with their implementation dependencies. for tsort. But I can't combine the test tasks since that would exceed the 4-requirement limit—testing all 5 SRD sub-requirements in a single task violates the constraint. So I'm settling on 4 tasks total: two implementation tasks splitting the requirements, and two separate test tasks that each stay within the limit.\n\nThe key dependency issue is that tests for R2.x features need the R1.x implementation to be in place first, and per the project rules, tests should follow implementation. So Task 0 handles the core R1.x implementation, Task 1 adds the R2.x implementation, then Tasks 2 and 3 test each layer respectively. I'm working through the canonical ordering for these tasks—implementation should come before tests within each requirement group, but across groups the primary file path determines the order. Since all tasks are in cmd/tsort/, I need to sequence the implementation tasks first, then the test tasks, respecting their dependencies. need R1 implementation. Tests for R2 need both R1 and R2 implementation. So task 2 depends on task 0, and task 3 depends on task 1. Since the dependency field only takes a single index, I'm setting it up as: task 0 for R1 implementation with no dependency, task 1 for R2 implementation depending on 0, task 2 for R1 tests depending on 0, and task 3 for R2 tests depending on 1.\n\nBut wait, should I really allocate all 4 tasks to tsort? The planning guidelines say to focus on the earliest incomplete release, so let me reconsider whether this is the best use of my task budget.\n\nRelease 12.1 is the earliest with ready requirements, so I should prioritize it. But I'm wondering if I can be more efficient with my task allocation. Combining all five requirements into one task exceeds the 4-requirement limit, so that won't work. I could split them as (R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R2.2) and (R2.3), but that leaves the second task with just one requirement, which feels inefficient. I'm exploring other splits like (R1.2, R1.3, R2.2, R2.3) with (R1.4) separate, or (R1.2, R1.3, R1.4) with (R2.2, R2.3) as the second group.\n\nThe policy says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but it also allows combining them if types and interfaces are small enough. For cmd/ packages without exported types, there's really just implementation plus tests. For something simple like tsort, I could potentially fit both into one task, but the policy explicitly requires splitting them. I'm torn between following the strict rule and being efficient with my limited task budget, especially since the differential tests in the _test.go files are really part of the verification pattern anyway.\n\nI'll stick with splitting them. Now I need to write out the four tasks, making sure to reference the SRD file at docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml, the existing code in cmd/tsort/, the test framework in pkg/testutils/, and the SIGPIPE handler in pkg/sys/. I should also check whether cmd/tsort/ already has some code from earlier work.\n\nLet me draft the task descriptions. Task 0 covers R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 in the main implementation with a weight of 3. Task 1 handles R2.2 and R2.3, also weight 2, and depends on task 0. Task 2 creates the differential tests for the first set of requirements, weight 3, depending on task 0. Task 3 adds tests for the second set, weight 2, depending on task 1.\n\nActually, I should reconsider whether a contract task is needed here. Since cmd/tsort is a command package with only a main function and internal implementation, not a pkg/ package that exports public APIs, I don't think a contract task applies.\n\nLooking at the git history and requirement states more carefully—the reset message suggests tsort requirements were stuck but got moved to ready status, though R1.1 and R2.1 aren't in that ready list, which might mean they were already completed. I should just instruct the stitch agent to check what code already exists and create or modify files as needed. I'm realizing the stitch agent will handle the file existence details, so I should just focus on writing clear task descriptions. Let me verify the constraint about \"ready\" requirements applies here—it does, since all these requirements are marked ready. Looking at the srd102-tsort requirements more carefully, I see R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 are ready in the R1 group, and R2.2, R2.3 are ready in the R2 group, with R1.1 and R2.1 likely already complete. SRD. I'm sketching out a task structure with four tasks: implementation for requirements R1.2-R1.4, implementation for R2.2-R2.3, then differential tests for each group. But I'm realizing the test task might exceed the four sub-requirement limit if I combine all five requirements into one test task, so I need to think about whether to split tests by requirement group or find another way to organize them.\n\nI've settled on keeping tests split by requirement group—Task 2 handles tests for R1.2-R1.4 and Task 3 handles tests for R2.2-R2.3, with appropriate dependencies. Now I'm moving into writing the actual YAML structure for each task, making sure the description field includes all required sections like deliverable_type, required_reading, files, requirements, and acceptance_criteria.\n\nWriting YAML task definitions... I'm realizing this approach is too abstract—the stitch agent won't know what R1.2, R1.3, and R1.4 actually specify. I should either embed the requirement text directly or instruct the agent to read from the SRD file since it's already listed in the required reading section. The planning constitution requires each SRD requirement to map to exactly one task requirement, so my mapping is correct. Tsort performs topological sorting with cycle detection, and the SRD likely groups requirements into core functionality (reading pairs and producing sorted output) and error handling (cycle detection and validation). Since R1.1 and R2.1 are already done, the remaining requirements extend these areas. I need to make sure each task is independently executable by an agent with only the task description and execution constitution—no assumptions about access to my analysis or prior context.\n\nThe stitch agent will read the required_reading files first, which contain the SRD with all necessary context about tsort, so I don't need to duplicate that information in the task descriptions. For the test tasks, I should include pkg/testutils/ in required_reading to reference the differential testing pattern the agent needs to follow.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the task dependencies: Task 2 tests R1.x functionality which only needs Task 0 (the R1.x implementation), while Task 3 tests R2.x functionality which requires both Task 0 and Task 1 since R2.x builds on R1.x. Since both test tasks modify the same test file, I need to make sure the dependencies are set up correctly so Task 3 runs after Task 1 completes. I'm realizing the dependency structure needs to respect the canonical ordering principle—all implementations should come before all tests. So I'll arrange it as: implement R1.x, implement R2.x, test R1.x, test R2.x, with task 2 depending on task 1 to ensure R2.x implementation uses the completed R1.x code, and task 3 depending on task 2 so the test file exists before R2.x tests run. I'm realizing the constraint here—with only a single dependency field per task, I need to chain them linearly so that task 3 automatically waits for everything it needs. If task 2 depends on task 1, and task 3 depends on task 2, then task 3 transitively depends on both implementations and the test file.\n\nSo the chain is: 0 → 1 → 2 → 3, where task 2 tests R1.x after both implementations are done, and task 3 tests R2.x after that. Even though task 2 doesn't strictly need the R2.x implementation, the ordering is safe and simple.\n\nNow I need to format the YAML output with proper task titles that anchor each one to specific SRD requirement IDs, like \"cmd/tsort implementation (srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\".\n\nFor the required_reading, I'm thinking about what each task actually needs—the SRD spec itself, any existing code files, and project conventions. The stitch agent already pulls CLAUDE.md automatically, so I don't need to list that explicitly.\n\nFor the implementation tasks, I need the SRD and the main.go file (either the initial version or with accumulated changes). For the test tasks, I also need the differential testing framework and any tests written in previous tasks. Let me verify what files actually exist in the project structure before finalizing these lists. I need to make sure I'm only documenting patterns that are actually derived from the SRD and architecture, not inventing anything. The differential testing approach, the SIGPIPE handler call in main, and the RunDiffTests pattern with BuildBinary and LookPath are all established in the codebase. For the reference binary, I'm checking the project convention where GNU coreutils tools use the \"g\" prefix through Homebrew, so tsort would be \"gtsort\". Now I'm ready to structure the YAML output for the tsort implementation task.\n\nWriting YAML structure... Looking at the next phase, I need to extend the existing implementation to add R2.2 and R2.3 functionality to the tsort command. The same build and vet checks apply, along with verifying that both new requirements are fully implemented. I'm laying out the test structure with three main requirements to cover: R1.2 for the first set of differential tests, R1.3 for the second, and R1.4 for the third. The design approach uses the testutils package to compile the tsort binary and compare it against the GNU reference implementation, skipping the test if gtsort isn't available. I need to define test cases as DiffTest structs and pass them to RunDiffTests, with acceptance criteria around running the full test suite successfully.\n\nNow I'm moving to the next phase which focuses on requirements R2.2 and R2.3, building on the differential testing pattern I just established. The acceptance criteria specify that the test suite should pass with `go test ./cmd/tsort/`, cover all the R2.2 and R2.3 behaviors, and integrate cleanly with existing tests. Let me verify the weight budget — Task 0 uses 3 weight units across its three requirements, and Task 1 uses 2 units for R2.2 and R2.3, both well within the 4-unit limit.\n\nI'm realizing I don't actually have access to requirements.yaml to confirm the exact weights, so I'm working with the assumption that each requirement has a default weight of 1. Some requirements might be weighted higher, but without being able to read that file, I'll note this assumption and proceed with the planning. +1 = 4 ✓ or 1+2 = 3 ✓\n\nSo my current task breakdown should be fine unless there's a requirement with weight 3 or higher, which seems unlikely given the project restructured those into separate groups. I'll move forward with this assumption.\n\nNow I need to finalize the YAML output and make sure it's properly formatted. Actually, wait—I should clarify the constraint about splitting implementation and tests. It sounds like the rule is to keep implementation code and test code in separate tasks, not necessarily that every requirement needs both. So if a requirement is just about implementation, it goes in one task; if there are tests, they go in another.\n\nThat's what I'm already doing, so I'm good there. For test tasks that reference SRD requirements, those requirements do count toward the weight budget, so I need to make sure I'm staying within the 4-point limit.\n\nI'm ready to write the final output now. Let me verify the YAML format is correct, especially with the literal block scalar for descriptions and proper indentation.\n\nI should stick to what the SRD actually specifies rather than speculating about what each requirement covers—the stitch agent will read the SRD directly anyway.\n\nLet me verify I'm following all the constraints: all four tasks target R-items with \"ready\" status, I have exactly 4 tasks which is the maximum, and each task references at most 3 sub-requirements. I'm also checking that I haven't proposed any R-items marked as complete, complete_with_failures, or skip.\n\nNow I need to finalize the task descriptions. For the tsort implementation, since R1.1 is likely already complete based on the git history, I should mark the main.go file as \"modify\" rather than \"create\". Though the stitch agent can handle creating it if needed, using modify is more accurate given the context that a previous task may have been reset. I'll use \"action: create\" for the test file since I'm not certain whether tests were already generated from the earlier requirements, and the stitch agent can handle creating it if needed. I'm going back and forth on whether to use \"create\" or \"modify\" for files that might already exist. The safest approach is to use \"create\" for the test file since I'm not certain it exists yet, and \"modify\" for the main implementation file since earlier tasks likely created it. The action field is really just a hint anyway — the agent will handle existing files appropriately regardless.\n\nActually, I should just commit to \"modify\" across the board. That way the agent will read existing content if it's there or create new if needed, without any risk of overwriting. I'll add a note to the test task clarifying that it should extend the existing file or create one if necessary.\n\nNow I need to focus on the actual output format, particularly getting the task titles right with the requirement IDs included as specified in the constraints.\n\nWriting the YAML task structure... Now I'm looking at the next task, which involves implementing two more requirements (R2.2 and R2.3) for the tsort command by modifying the existing main.go file to add the specified functionality. Now I'm looking at the test structure I need to build—I'll be adding differential test cases to cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go that compare my implementation against the GNU gtsort reference binary for the R1.2, R1.3, and R1.4 behaviors, using the test utilities available in pkg/testutils. R3\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R1.4 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use testutils.BuildBinary(t, \\\".\\\") to compile cmd/tsort and exec.LookPath(\\\"gtsort\\\") for the reference binary\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call t.Skip if gtsort is not found in PATH per project convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Define test cases as []testutils.DiffTest and pass to testutils.RunDiffTests\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Set LC_ALL=C in test environment per project convention\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/tsort/ -count=1 passes when gtsort is available, or skips gracefully when it is not\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Test cases cover all behaviors specified in srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests follow the differential testing pattern: BuildBinary, LookPath, RunDiffTests\"\n\nNow I'm moving on to the next task: adding differential tests for R2.2 and R2.3 requirements, which will follow the same pattern but exercise the newer specification behaviors. I need to review the updated requirements in the spec file and the existing test structure to design appropriate test cases. I'm verifying the task structure against all the constraints—checking that everything targets the right release, requirements are properly anchored, implementation and tests are separated, we're within the task and weight limits, dependencies chain correctly, and each description is complete and self-contained. This looks solid, so I'll move on to writing the summary.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44254,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44254},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fba7a459-f8a4-4e82-b81f-594266b74153","uuid":"03676747-49cb-40d0-9e60-57a2753a0380"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0184iwXWVcCnuBTM1qB3aUkH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: GNU utilities lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. This project derives specifications from C source, generates Go implementations via an AI pipeline, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify). `docs/specs/` holds SRDs and use cases. `cmd/<name>/` holds one Go binary per utility. `pkg/` provides shared infrastructure (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil). `magefiles/` drives the build and generation pipeline.\n\n**Current state**: 111 utilities specified across 33 releases (00.0 through 15.0). Many cmd/ packages are implemented; generation run 44 is in progress. The `requirement_states` field shows which specific requirements are ready for task proposals.\n\n**Current release focus**: Release 12.1 (tsort, pathchk, test) is the earliest release with requirements in \"ready\" state. Specifically, `srd102-tsort` has 5 ready requirements: R1.2, R1.3, R1.4, R2.2, R2.3. Requirements R1.1 and R2.1 are not listed as \"ready\", indicating they were completed in prior tasks.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest release with ready R-items is **12.1** (`srd102-tsort`). Other ready requirements exist in releases 13.0 (dircolors), 14.0 (chronic, pee, vidir), and 15.0 (ts, stty, pr, ptx), but release-driven priority dictates we finish 12.1 first.\n\nPer P2, implementation and tests must be separate tasks. With 5 SRD sub-requirements and a limit of 4 per task, I split by requirement group: R1.x (3 reqs) and R2.x (2 reqs), each getting an implementation task and a test task.\n\n## Weight Budget Verification\n\nAssuming default weight 1 (cannot read requirements.yaml per no-tools constraint):\n\n- Task 0: srd102-tsort R1.2 (w=1), R1.3 (w=1), R1.4 (w=1) → Total: 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 1: srd102-tsort R2.2 (w=1), R2.3 (w=1) → Total: 2 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 2: Tests for R1.2 (w=1), R1.3 (w=1), R1.4 (w=1) → Total: 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 3: Tests for R2.2 (w=1), R2.3 (w=1) → Total: 2 ≤ 4 ✓\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/tsort implementation (srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (existing implementation from completed requirements R1.1 and R2.1)\n      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler pattern)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Extend tsort implementation to cover requirements R1.2, R1.3, R1.4\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort requirement R1.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort requirement R1.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort requirement R1.4 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Extend the existing cmd/tsort/main.go -- do not rewrite code from previously completed requirements (R1.1, R2.1)\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per project convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Build with: go build -o bin/tsort ./cmd/tsort/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build -o bin/tsort ./cmd/tsort/ compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/tsort/ reports no issues\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All behaviors specified in srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 are implemented in cmd/tsort/main.go\"\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"cmd/tsort implementation (srd102-tsort R2.2, R2.3)\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R2.2, R2.3)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (existing implementation including R1.x requirements)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Add R2.2 and R2.3 functionality to tsort\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort requirement R2.2 as specified in the SRD\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd102-tsort requirement R2.3 as specified in the SRD\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Extend the existing cmd/tsort/main.go -- do not rewrite code from previously completed requirements\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Build with: go build -o bin/tsort ./cmd/tsort/\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go build -o bin/tsort ./cmd/tsort/ compiles without errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go vet ./cmd/tsort/ reports no issues\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All behaviors specified in srd102-tsort R2.2, R2.3 are implemented in cmd/tsort/main.go\"\n\n- index: 2\n  title: \"cmd/tsort differential tests (srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4)\"\n  dependency: 1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 for test case design)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (implementation under test)\n      - cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go (existing test file if present)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (RunDiffTests, BuildBinary, DiffTest struct)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Add differential test cases covering R1.2, R1.3, R1.4 behaviors\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R1.2 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R1.3 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R1.4 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use testutils.BuildBinary(t, \\\".\\\") to compile cmd/tsort and exec.LookPath(\\\"gtsort\\\") for the reference binary\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call t.Skip if gtsort is not found in PATH per project convention\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Define test cases as []testutils.DiffTest and pass to testutils.RunDiffTests\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Set LC_ALL=C in test environment per project convention\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/tsort/ -count=1 passes when gtsort is available, or skips gracefully when it is not\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Test cases cover all behaviors specified in srd102-tsort R1.2, R1.3, R1.4\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests follow the differential testing pattern: BuildBinary, LookPath, RunDiffTests\"\n\n- index: 3\n  title: \"cmd/tsort differential tests (srd102-tsort R2.2, R2.3)\"\n  dependency: 2\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd102-tsort.yaml (requirements R2.2, R2.3 for test case design)\n      - cmd/tsort/main.go (implementation under test)\n      - cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go (existing test file with R1.x test cases)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (RunDiffTests, BuildBinary, DiffTest struct)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/tsort/tsort_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: \"Add differential test cases covering R2.2, R2.3 behaviors\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R2.2 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Add differential test cases exercising srd102-tsort R2.3 behaviors against the GNU gtsort reference binary\"\n\n    design_decision